    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The distribution of joint-based versus deformation band-based faulting mechanisms varies along the Moab fault in Jurassic eolian sandstone units. Because each mechanism dominates in specific regions defined by proximity to fault tips, relays, and intersections in several different sandstone units, this distribution pattern is hypothesized to be mechanically controlled. This study aims to relate the distribution of faulting mechanisms to the local state of stress at different locations around the Moab normal fault system. The mechanical analysis of the stress perturbation around the fault demonstrates that joints occur in locations with reduced compression, measured as mean stress or the magnitude of least compressive stress, consistent with joint formation in local effective tension. Furthermore, the orientations of joints predicted from the mechanical models correspond to the orientation of joints measured in the field.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
